"""Year summaries, 2001-2025. See _year_summaries_1926_1950.py for notes on sourcing and accuracy. Recent-year figures are approximate."""
S = {
2001: "A recession began in March as the dot-com bust fed through to investment and jobs, and the Fed cut rates eleven times during the year, from 6.5 to 1.75 percent. The September 11 attacks closed the NYSE for four sessions, the longest shutdown since 1933, and the Dow fell about 14 percent in its first week back before recovering over the following weeks. Enron, once the seventh-largest US company, filed for bankruptcy in December after accounting fraud was exposed, undermining trust in corporate reporting. China joined the World Trade Organization in December, reshaping global supply chains. Argentina's economy was collapsing under its currency peg and defaulted at year end. The S&P 500 lost about 13 percent for the year.",
2002: "The bear market reached its low on 9 October, when the S&P 500 closed near 777, down about 49 percent from its March 2000 peak, and the Nasdaq fell roughly 78 percent. Accounting scandals at WorldCom, Tyco and Adelphia led to the Sarbanes-Oxley Act in July, tightening corporate governance and auditor oversight. The S&P 500 lost about 23 percent, its third straight annual decline, the first such run since the 1939-41 period. The Fed trimmed rates to 1.25 percent in November. The euro entered circulation in January, and the dollar began a multi-year decline. Concerns about Iraq and weak corporate earnings weighed on sentiment, while housing began to take over as the engine of consumer spending.",
2003: "The market made its low on 11 March, as the Iraq War started, and then rallied strongly, with the S&P 500 gaining about 26 percent and the Nasdaq about 50 percent for the year. The Fed cut rates to 1 percent in June, the lowest in 45 years, and stressed that policy would stay accommodative for a considerable period, while the Bush tax cuts reduced taxes on dividends and capital gains. Fears of deflation prompted the central bank's unusual stance. Mortgage lending and refinancing boomed. The SARS outbreak hit Asian economies in the spring. Oil prices rose, the dollar kept weakening, and the economy gradually began adding jobs. Investors rotated back into risk.",
2004: "The Federal Reserve started a measured tightening cycle on 30 June, raising rates by a quarter point at each of 17 consecutive meetings through June 2006 to 5.25 percent. Google went public on 19 August in a Dutch auction, and Mark Zuckerberg launched Facebook from his Harvard dorm in February. Equities advanced modestly, with the S&P 500 up about 9 percent, while oil climbed above $50 a barrel on strong Chinese demand. The housing boom accelerated, helped by adjustable-rate and subprime loans, and credit was easily available. The Indian Ocean tsunami struck in December. Bush won reelection, and the dollar slid further as the current account deficit widened.",
2005: "Housing boom conditions peaked in many regions, with prices rising by double digits and speculative buying common, while lenders relaxed standards on subprime and interest-only mortgages. Fed chairman Alan Greenspan described long-term yields staying low despite rate hikes as a 'conundrum'. In July China ended the hard peg of the yuan to the dollar, allowing a small appreciation. Hurricane Katrina struck the Gulf Coast in August, flooding New Orleans and disrupting oil production, and gasoline prices spiked. The S&P 500 gained only about 3 percent. Ben Bernanke was nominated to succeed Greenspan, whose term ended in January 2006. Corporate profits were strong, and emerging-market and commodity prices rose.",
2006: "US house prices peaked around mid-year and began to fall, an early sign of trouble in the market for subprime mortgages and the securities built on them. Ben Bernanke became Fed chair on 1 February, and the Fed ended its 17 rate hikes in June at 5.25 percent, then held. The S&P 500 gained about 14 percent and the Dow set records, with equities supported by strong earnings and takeover activity. The yield curve inverted, a recession warning that was largely dismissed at the time. Oil climbed above $75 in the summer before falling. Hedge fund Amaranth lost billions on natural gas. Subprime lenders began to show rising delinquencies, and the first lenders failed.",
2007: "The credit crunch began. On 9 August BNP Paribas froze three funds invested in US mortgage securities, citing the impossibility of valuing them, and interbank lending seized up, with central banks injecting liquidity. In September, depositors queued outside Northern Rock in Britain, the first bank run there in over a century. The Fed began cutting rates in September, and Apple launched the iPhone in June. The Dow peaked on 9 October at about 14,164 and the S&P 500 hit a high in October, though the year ended with only modest gains. Bear Stearns hedge funds had failed in the summer, and banks such as Citigroup and Merrill Lynch reported huge subprime write-downs. Oil neared $100.",
2008: "The global financial crisis. Bear Stearns was rescued by JPMorgan with Fed support in March, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac were placed in conservatorship in September, and on 15 September Lehman Brothers filed for the largest bankruptcy in US history. The Fed rescued AIG, the money-market fund Reserve Primary broke the buck, and credit markets froze worldwide. Congress passed the $700 billion TARP bank rescue package in October, and the Fed cut rates to near zero in December and began quantitative easing. The S&P 500 fell about 38 percent in the year, its worst since 1931, and the US lost millions of jobs. Oil spiked to $147 in July then collapsed to about $35.",
2009: "The S&P 500 hit a low of 666 on 9 March, down about 57 percent from its 2007 peak, then began one of the strongest bull markets in history, ending the year up about 23 percent. The Fed expanded its balance sheet through its first round of quantitative easing, buying mortgage securities and Treasuries, and kept rates near zero, while Congress passed the $787 billion stimulus package in February. Bank stress tests in spring restored confidence in the financial system, and the recession officially ended in June, though unemployment peaked at 10 percent in October. Bitcoin's genesis block was mined on 3 January. General Motors and Chrysler entered bankruptcy. Greece's hidden deficits foreshadowed the euro crisis.",
2010: "The eurozone sovereign debt crisis began as Greece's deficits were revealed and borrowing costs soared, leading to a €110 billion EU-IMF rescue in May and a further rescue for Ireland in November. On 6 May the 'Flash Crash' saw the Dow fall nearly 1,000 points within minutes before recovering, highlighting the fragility of electronic markets. The Dodd-Frank Act was signed in July to reform financial regulation, and the Fed announced a second round of quantitative easing, QE2, in November, buying $600 billion of Treasuries. The S&P 500 rose about 13 percent despite a spring correction. The BP Deepwater Horizon oil spill in April was a major environmental disaster. China overtook Japan as the second-largest economy.",
2011: "Standard & Poor's downgraded the US credit rating from AAA to AA+ on 5 August after a bitter debt-ceiling standoff, yet Treasury yields fell as investors sought safety. Gold peaked at about $1,900 an ounce in September. The eurozone crisis deepened, with Italy and Spain under pressure and Greece restructuring its debt, and global stocks swung widely, with the S&P 500 finishing flat. The Arab Spring toppled governments in Tunisia, Egypt and Libya, the Tohoku earthquake and tsunami in March devastated Japan and disrupted supply chains, and MF Global failed in October. The Fed launched Operation Twist to lower long-term rates. Steve Jobs died in October.",
2012: "The eurozone stabilised after ECB president Mario Draghi pledged on 26 July to do 'whatever it takes' to preserve the euro, and in September the ECB announced its Outright Monetary Transactions programme, which eased bond spreads without being used. The Fed launched an open-ended QE3 programme in September, buying mortgage securities, and the S&P 500 gained about 13 percent. Facebook went public on 18 May at a $104 billion valuation, with a troubled debut. The LIBOR rate-fixing scandal emerged, JPMorgan's 'London Whale' trading loss surfaced, and Hurricane Sandy struck New York in October. Barack Obama won reelection, and the US faced the 'fiscal cliff' at year end.",
2013: "The 'taper tantrum' began in May and June when Fed chairman Bernanke hinted that asset purchases could slow, causing Treasury yields to jump from about 1.6 to 3 percent by year end, bond funds to suffer outflows, and emerging-market currencies such as the rupee and real to slide. The Fed formally began tapering in December. The S&P 500 gained about 30 percent, its best year since 1997, driven by multiple expansion. Bitcoin rose above $1,000 in November for the first time, and the US government shut down for 16 days in October. Detroit filed for the largest municipal bankruptcy in US history in July, and Janet Yellen was nominated to lead the Fed.",
2014: "Oil prices collapsed from above $100 in June to about $55 by December as US shale output surged and OPEC chose not to cut production, hurting energy producers and exporters such as Russia, whose rouble crashed, while helping consumers. Mt. Gox, then the largest bitcoin exchange, collapsed in February, and Alibaba's IPO in September raised about $25 billion, the largest ever. The Fed ended its QE3 asset purchases in October, while the ECB and Bank of Japan moved to ease further, causing the dollar to surge. The S&P 500 gained about 11 percent, and Treasury yields fell despite expectations of the opposite. Russia annexed Crimea in March, bringing sanctions.",
2015: "China surprised markets on 11 August by devaluing the yuan, triggering global volatility, and Chinese stocks fell sharply after a bubble, with the S&P 500 suffering a flash drop on 24 August. Commodity prices kept falling and energy companies suffered, with high-yield credit under strain. The Fed raised rates on 16 December for the first time since 2006, lifting the funds rate target to 0.25 to 0.5 percent, ending seven years of zero policy. The S&P 500 finished about flat, with narrow leadership from a handful of big technology names. The Greek crisis peaked in the summer, with capital controls and a referendum, and the Volkswagen emissions scandal broke in September.",
2016: "Political shocks dominated. Britain voted to leave the European Union on 23 June, the pound fell sharply and global markets dropped before recovering quickly, and Donald Trump's surprise victory on 8 November triggered a rally in banks and cyclicals and a jump in Treasury yields, the 'reflation trade'. Oil fell to a low of about $26 in February before recovering to above $50 after OPEC agreed its first cut in eight years. The S&P 500 gained about 10 percent. The Fed raised rates a second time in December. The initial coin offering boom started as Ethereum's DAO and token sales drew attention, and negative interest rates spread in Europe and Japan.",
2017: "A year of synchronised global growth and extraordinarily low volatility, with the VIX hitting record lows and the S&P 500 gaining about 19 percent without a drawdown of more than 3 percent. Bitcoin surged from about $1,000 to nearly $20,000 in December as the crypto mania drew retail investors and initial coin offerings. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act in December cut the corporate tax rate to 21 percent. The Fed raised rates three times and announced the start of balance sheet reduction. The FAANG stocks led the market, and passive funds attracted record inflows. Equifax's data breach and the Harvey and Maria hurricanes were notable shocks.",
2018: "Volatility returned. On 5 February 'Volmageddon' saw the VIX more than double in a day, wiping out inverse-volatility products such as XIV, and US stocks fell about 10 percent in a few days. The trade war with China escalated through tariffs, and emerging markets such as Turkey and Argentina suffered currency crises. The Fed continued raising rates four times and shrinking its balance sheet, and Fed chair Jerome Powell's remarks on being 'a long way' from neutral in October preceded a sharp Q4 sell-off, with the S&P 500 falling about 20 percent from September to Christmas Eve and ending the year down about 6 percent. Bitcoin lost about 70 percent.",
2019: "The Fed pivoted from tightening to easing, cutting rates three times from July to October, and the S&P 500 gained about 29 percent as markets rebounded from the late-2018 sell-off. The yield curve inverted in the spring and summer, raising recession fears, while the US-China trade war flared again. In September overnight repo rates spiked, forcing the Fed to inject liquidity, and the WeWork IPO collapsed, signalling a shift in investor tolerance for money-losing startups. Uber and Lyft listed to disappointing results. Brexit uncertainty dominated Britain until the December election. Late in the year news of a new virus in Wuhan, China, began to emerge, though markets ignored it.",
2020: "The COVID-19 pandemic caused the fastest bear market in history, with the S&P 500 falling about 34 percent between 19 February and 23 March, and then recovering to new highs by August. Economies shut down, US unemployment reached 14.7 percent in April, and WTI crude futures fell below zero on 20 April. The Fed cut rates to zero, relaunched quantitative easing and opened lending facilities, while Congress passed the $2.2 trillion CARES Act, among the largest fiscal responses ever. Technology stocks surged, retail trading and SPACs boomed, and vaccines were announced in November. The S&P 500 finished up about 16 percent. Joe Biden won the presidential election.",
2021: "A year of liquidity-fuelled euphoria. Retail traders drove meme stocks such as GameStop and AMC in January, SPACs raised record sums, NFTs and crypto soared, with Bitcoin reaching about $69,000 in November, and the S&P 500 gained about 27 percent. Vaccines allowed reopening, supply chains were disrupted, and inflation rose sharply, with US CPI reaching about 7 percent by December, which the Fed called 'transitory' until November, when it began to taper. Fiscal stimulus of $1.9 trillion was passed in March. Archegos collapsed in March, costing banks billions, and Evergrande's debt troubles surfaced in September. Housing prices surged, and the Great Resignation reshaped labour markets.",
2022: "Russia invaded Ukraine on 24 February, driving energy and food prices up, and the Fed delivered the fastest rate hikes in decades, from near zero to 4.5 percent in 2022, with 75-basis-point moves, to fight inflation that peaked around 9 percent. Stocks and bonds fell together: the S&P 500 lost about 19 percent and the Nasdaq about 33 percent, while the aggregate US bond index lost around 13 percent. The Terra/Luna stablecoin collapsed in May and FTX failed in November, deepening the crypto crash, with Bitcoin falling roughly 65 percent. The UK gilt crisis followed the September mini-budget. ChatGPT was released on 30 November. The dollar surged to two-decade highs.",
2023: "Silicon Valley Bank failed on 10 March after a run, followed by Signature Bank and then First Republic, and Credit Suisse was forced into a merger with UBS, causing the worst US banking turmoil since 2008. The Fed responded with a new lending facility and continued hiking to 5.25 to 5.5 percent by July. Despite fears of recession, the economy proved resilient, and inflation fell. An AI boom, triggered by ChatGPT and led by Nvidia, drove the S&P 500 up about 24 percent and the Nasdaq about 43 percent, concentrated in a handful of mega-cap 'Magnificent Seven' stocks. OpenAI released GPT-4 in March. Treasury yields reached about 5 percent in October before falling.",
2024: "AI enthusiasm and a soft landing drove equities to repeated records, with the S&P 500 up about 23 percent and Nvidia passing a $3 trillion market value in June. US spot Bitcoin ETFs launched in January, and Bitcoin rose above $100,000 in December after Donald Trump's election win. On 5 August the unwinding of the yen carry trade after a Bank of Japan hike caused a brief global sell-off, with the VIX spiking above 60 intraday. The Fed began cutting rates in September with a half-point move, bringing the rate to 4.25 to 4.5 percent by December. Inflation eased toward 3 percent. Elections around the world and the Middle East conflicts dominated headlines.",
2025: "A volatile, narrative-driven year. In January, the DeepSeek AI model triggered a sharp one-day fall in Nvidia and other AI stocks, and on 2 April the 'Liberation Day' tariffs announcement drove a sharp sell-off, followed by a rebound after a pause and trade deals. Heavy investment in AI infrastructure by large technology companies lifted Nvidia to a market value of roughly $4 to 5 trillion, and gold climbed above $4,000 an ounce as central banks and investors sought safe havens. Bitcoin reached a record near $126,000 in October before pulling back. The Fed resumed cutting rates in the autumn. Debates grew about AI valuations and circular financing among suppliers and customers.",
}
